============================================================ QUESTION 1
– PYTHON BACKEND APPLICATION USING FLASK
============================================================

AIM

Create a Python Flask backend application that supports all four CRUD
operations for student records.

STEP 1: Create the project directory

mkdir student cd student

If the student directory already exists, use:

cd ~/student

STEP 2: Create app.py

nano app.py

Paste the complete code below:

  --------------
  FILE: app.py
  --------------

from flask import Flask, request, jsonify

app = Flask(name)

Two initial student records

students = [ {“id”: 101, “name”: “Roji”}, {“id”: 102, “name”:
“Akshaya”}]

CREATE: Add a new student

@app.route(‘/students’, methods=[‘POST’]) def create_student(): data =
request.get_json()

    if not data or "id" not in data or "name" not in data:
        return jsonify({"message": "Student ID and name are required"}), 400

    if any(student["id"] == data["id"] for student in students):
        return jsonify({"message": "Student ID already exists"}), 409

    student = {
        "id": data["id"],
        "name": data["name"]
    }

    students.append(student)
    return jsonify(student), 201

READ: Get all students

@app.route(‘/students’, methods=[‘GET’]) def read_students(): return
jsonify(students), 200

UPDATE: Update a student’s name

@app.route(‘/students/’, methods=[‘PUT’]) def
update_student(student_id): data = request.get_json()

    if not data or "name" not in data:
        return jsonify({"message": "Student name is required"}), 400

    for student in students:
        if student["id"] == student_id:
            student["name"] = data["name"]
            return jsonify(student), 200

    return jsonify({"message": "Student not found"}), 404

DELETE: Delete a student

@app.route(‘/students/’, methods=[‘DELETE’]) def
delete_student(student_id): for student in students: if student[“id”] ==
student_id: students.remove(student) return jsonify({“message”: “Student
deleted successfully”}), 200

    return jsonify({"message": "Student not found"}), 404

if name == ‘main’: app.run(host=‘0.0.0.0’, port=5000)

  ---------------
  END OF app.py
  ---------------

Save nano: 1. Press Ctrl + O. 2. Press Enter. 3. Press Ctrl + X.

STEP 3: Install Python tools and create a virtual environment

On Ubuntu/Debian-based playgrounds, if Python or venv is missing:

apt update apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip curl

Create and activate the virtual environment:

python3 -m venv venv source venv/bin/activate

If the environment reports that externally-managed Python packages are
protected, use the virtual environment above and then install Flask.

STEP 4: Install Flask

python -m pip install flask

Check Flask:

python -m pip show flask

STEP 5: Run the Flask application

python app.py

Expected message includes:

-   Running on http://127.0.0.1:5000

Leave this terminal running.

STEP 6: Open a SECOND terminal

Use the terminal ‘+’ button in Killercoda. In the second terminal, run:

curl http://localhost:5000/students

Expected initial records:

[ {“id”: 101, “name”: “Roji”}, {“id”: 102, “name”: “Akshaya”}]

STEP 7: Test all CRUD operations

A. CREATE – POST

curl -X POST http://localhost:5000/students
-H “Content-Type: application/json”
-d ‘{“id”:103,“name”:“Divya”}’

Expected: student with ID 103 is returned.

B. READ – GET

curl http://localhost:5000/students

Expected: all current student records are returned.

C. UPDATE – PUT

curl -X PUT http://localhost:5000/students/101
-H “Content-Type: application/json”
-d ‘{“name”:“Roji Updated”}’

Expected:

{“id”:101,“name”:“Roji Updated”}

D. DELETE – DELETE

curl -X DELETE http://localhost:5000/students/102

Expected:

{“message”:“Student deleted successfully”}

Read the records again:

curl http://localhost:5000/students

NOTE: The records are stored in a Python list, not a database. They
reset when the Flask process restarts.

