============================================================ QUESTION 3
– DOCKER: BUILD AND RUN IMAGE/CONTAINER
============================================================

AIM

Create a Docker image from the local Flask application and run it as a
Docker container.

IMPORTANT: Use a Killercoda playground that has Docker installed and
available. The Kubernetes playground and Docker playground may be
separate environments. If they are separate, push the project to GitHub
first, then clone it in the Docker playground.

STEP 1: Check Docker

docker –version

If Docker is unavailable, open a Docker-enabled Killercoda playground.
Do not assume Docker can be installed or started in every playground.

STEP 2: Get the project

If this is a separate Docker playground, clone the GitHub repository:

git clone https://github.com/ro-ro48/devops-external1.git cd
devops-external1

If the project is already present, enter its folder instead:

cd ~/student

Check files:

ls

app.py must be present.

STEP 3: Create requirements.txt

echo flask > requirements.txt

Check:

cat requirements.txt

STEP 4: Create Dockerfile

nano Dockerfile

Paste the following. The file must be named exactly Dockerfile, with no
.txt extension.

  ------------------
  FILE: Dockerfile
  ------------------

FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install –no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY app.py .

EXPOSE 5000

CMD [“python”, “app.py”]

  -------------------
  END OF Dockerfile
  -------------------

Save with Ctrl + O, Enter, Ctrl + X.

STEP 5: Build the image

docker build -t student-app .

The dot at the end is required. It means the current directory is the
Docker build context.

STEP 6: Verify the image

docker images

Look for the image named student-app.

STEP 7: Run a container

docker run -d -p 5001:5000 –name student-container student-app

This maps host port 5001 to the Flask application’s container port 5000.

STEP 8: Verify the running container

docker ps

Look for student-container with an Up status.

STEP 9: Test the application inside the container

curl http://localhost:5001/students

Expected initial records:

[ {“id”: 101, “name”: “Roji”}, {“id”: 102, “name”: “Akshaya”}]

You can also test POST:

curl -X POST http://localhost:5001/students
-H “Content-Type: application/json”
-d ‘{“id”:103,“name”:“Divya”}’

STEP 10: View container logs if needed

docker logs student-container

STEP 11: Stop and remove the container

docker stop student-container docker rm student-container

Optional: remove the image after the practical:

docker rmi student-app

