============================================================ QUESTION 4
– KUBERNETES DEPLOYMENT USING deployment.yaml
============================================================

AIM

Create and configure a Kubernetes Deployment using a deployment.yaml
file.

IMPORTANT: Return to the Killercoda Kubernetes playground. The terminal
should be connected to the Kubernetes control plane.

STEP 1: Check kubectl

kubectl version –client

STEP 2: Check cluster nodes

kubectl get nodes

Continue when the node status is Ready.

STEP 3: Create deployment.yaml

You can create this file in the current home directory:

nano deployment.yaml

Paste the following YAML. Indentation matters; use spaces, not tabs.

  -----------------------
  FILE: deployment.yaml
  -----------------------

apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: student-deployment
spec: replicas: 2 selector: matchLabels: app: student template:
metadata: labels: app: student spec: containers: - name:
student-container image: nginx ports: - containerPort: 80

  ------------------------
  END OF deployment.yaml
  ------------------------

Save with Ctrl + O, Enter, Ctrl + X.

STEP 4: Verify the file

cat deployment.yaml

STEP 5: Apply the deployment

kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Expected:

deployment.apps/student-deployment created

STEP 6: Check the deployment

kubectl get deployments

Wait until READY shows 2/2.

STEP 7: Check Pods

kubectl get pods

Wait until both Pods show Running and READY 1/1.

If they are not ready immediately, run kubectl get pods again after a
short wait. If a Pod fails, inspect it using:

kubectl describe pods

STEP 8: Describe the deployment

kubectl describe deployment student-deployment

STEP 9: View all related resources

kubectl get all

STEP 10: Optional cleanup

kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml

